        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Poltava</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">8 July 1709 (28 June in the Swedish calendar)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Near the fortress of Poltava on the Vorskla river, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Russian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Peter I, 75,000 to 80,000 men (80,000 in another account of the build-up), against a Swedish army under Field Marshal Carl Gustaf Rehnskiöld, with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> wounded, which had been reduced to 20,000 soldiers and 34 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> by the spring</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Swedes were defeated and fled the field. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mazepa">Ivan Mazepa</a> escaped with about 1,500 men, and the rest of the army surrendered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulation-at-perevolochna">Perevolochna</a> on 11 July 1709.</span></div>
