    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Swedish army had shrunk to 20,000 soldiers and 34 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> by spring 1709, after a campaign through Russian scorched earth and the coldest winter in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in over 500 years, while Peter brought 75,000 to 80,000 men.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> was wounded in the foot on 20 June and could not stand, so Field Marshal Rehnskiöld led the attack, and about a third of the Swedish infantry under Roos was cut off at the redoubts before the main fight began.</li><li>Charles and Mazepa escaped to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> with about 1,500 men, and the surrender at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulation-at-perevolochna">Perevolochna</a> three days later ended the Swedish army as a field force.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> spent five years in exile at Bendery in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> after Poltava, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> only in December 1715.</p></aside>
    </section>

