    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1700</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> wins early victories in North Zealand and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1706</span><span class="rf-text">Peter I withdraws from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and offers to return the occupied Baltic provinces, except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a>; Charles refuses</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1707</span><span class="rf-text">The Swedish army of almost 44,000 leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in August</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1708</span><span class="rf-text">Charles turns south into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> to join Mazepa, and Lewenhaupt loses his supply train at Lesnaya</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1709</span><span class="rf-text">Charles besieges Poltava from 2 May and is wounded in the foot on 20 June</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1709</span><span class="rf-text">The Swedes attack the Russian redoubts before dawn and are defeated in the open field</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1709</span><span class="rf-text">Lewenhaupt surrenders the remaining Swedes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulation-at-perevolochna">Perevolochna</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1715</span><span class="rf-text">Charles returns to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in December after five years in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-poltava" data-v="7f93fffac0" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Swedes form south of the redoubts</b> The Swedish columns formed in the night 600 metres south of the redoubts, with the Russian cavalry behind the redoubts and the camp beyond. The infantry was in place early, but the cavalry arrived late.</li><li><b>Roos overruns two redoubts and stalls at the third</b> The Swedes in Roos' column quickly overran the first two redoubts and killed every Russian soldier inside them. By 4:30 a.m. their attempts to take the third redoubt had stalled.</li><li><b>The Swedish wings pass the redoubts</b> Lewenhaupt's ten battalions on the right bypassed the first four redoubts. Hamilton's cavalry on the left passed the redoubts and charged the Russian cavalry, forcing it to retreat.</li><li><b>Lewenhaupt is ordered back to the west</b> Rehnskiöld ordered his cavalry to stop the pursuit and Lewenhaupt to withdraw to the west. There the wings waited for Roos' battalions for two hours.</li><li><b>Roos takes his men into the Yakovetski woods</b> Roos and six battalions, a third of the Swedish infantry, had become isolated at the third redoubt. After severe casualties he led the remaining 1,500 of his original 2,600 men into the Yakovetski woods to the east.</li><li><b>The Russians attack Roos and drive him back</b> The Russians reoccupied the first two redoubts and launched a two-pronged attack by ten regiments. Roos was forced to retreat towards Poltava.</li><li><b>Roos takes refuge in a fort and surrenders</b> Roos could not reach the Swedish siege works and took refuge in an abandoned fort by 9:00 a.m. He was forced to surrender at 9:30 a.m.</li><li><b>Peter leads the Russian infantry out of the camp</b> Peter led his infantry out of the fortified camp, with dragoons under Bauer on his right and cavalry under Menshikov on his left. West of the camp they faced the Swedish infantry, with Creutz's cavalry in the rear.</li><li><b>The Swedes advance and the Russian first line gives way</b> Rehnskiöld ordered Lewenhaupt and the Swedish line forward. The Swedes fired a volley, charged with muskets and pikemen, and the Russian first line retreated towards its second line.</li><li><b>The Swedish left flank breaks</b> The Russian line was longer, and the Swedish infantry on the left flank lagged behind the right and finally threw down their weapons and fled. A gap opened in the Swedish line and the Russians filled it.</li><li><b>Creutz is checked and Menshikov takes the Swedes in the rear</b> Barely able to gather his squadrons, Creutz tried to advance on the right flank, but the Russian battalions formed hollow squares. Menshikov's cavalry outflanked the Swedes and attacked them from the rear.</li><li><b>The Swedish assault disintegrates</b> Small groups of Swedes broke through and escaped to the south through the Budyschenski woods, while many of the rest were overwhelmed, ridden down or captured. Charles ordered a retreat to the woods with what forces he could gather.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden.jpg" alt="Charles XII of Sweden" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII of Sweden</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copy_Charles_XII_-_Nationalmuseum_-_17886.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Workshop of Hyacinthe Rigaud, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> led Swedish forces to early victories in North Zealand in summer 1700 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> in November 1700. It took him six years to defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong">Augustus II</a> of Saxony-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Peter I withdrew from Poland in the spring of 1706 and offered to return the Baltic provinces that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had occupied since 1703, except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a>, where he had begun to build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St. Petersburg</a>. Charles refused, and Peter adopted a scorched-earth policy to deprive the Swedes of supplies. By then Russia was the only member of the original anti-Swedish coalition still fighting.</p><p>The Swedish army of almost 44,000 men left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> on 22 August 1707, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> on 25 December and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grodno">Grodno</a> on 26 January 1708 after the Russians abandoned it. The Russians were occupied with the Bulavin Rebellion of the Don <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mazepa">Ivan Mazepa</a> helped to contain. In July 1708 the Swedes beat Boris Sheremetyev at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-holowczyn">Holowczyn</a> and reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>.</p><p>General Lewenhaupt, ordered to bring supplies and about 12,000 men from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>, left late and joined Charles only on 11 October. Charles then chose to move south into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> to join Mazepa, who had decided to rebel against Peter. Lewenhaupt was attacked at Lesnaya and lost his supply train and half of his force. Aleksandr Menshikov reached Mazepa's headquarters at Baturyn first and ordered the population massacred and the city razed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the Great Frost of 1708 to 1709, called the coldest winter in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in over 500 years, Charles was left with 20,000 soldiers and 34 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>, about half the army's original size. He had been cut off from further help: the Swedish forces under von Krassow in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> had turned aside to deal with the Sandomierz Confederation. Short of supplies, he laid siege to the fortress of Poltava on 2 May 1709. Peter marched to relieve it with 80,000 men, a figure given elsewhere as 75,000 to 80,000, and built a fortified camp on the Vorskla 4 km north of the town.</p><p>On 20 June, while observing the Russian position, Charles was hit in the foot by a stray bullet and could not stand. He gave operational command to Field Marshal Carl Gustaf Rehnskiöld.</p><p>The Yakovetski and Budyschenski woods formed a corridor between the armies, which the Russians closed with six forts and four more redoubts, so that the ten works formed a T shape and could give flanking fire. Two redoubts were still unfinished on the morning of the battle. The other eight held 4,000 Russians, with 10,000 cavalry under Menshikov behind them. The Swedish plan was for four columns of infantry and six of cavalry to form during the night 600 metres south of the redoubts and attack before dawn, bypassing them to reach the Russian camp.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The infantry was in place by 2:30 a.m., but the cavalry lost its way and arrived late. Axel Gyllenkrok saw the Russians still working on the two nearest redoubts, and a reconnaissance by Schlippenbach was discovered and the alarm raised by a pistol shot. The surprise was lost and the Swedes lacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> to breach the works. After consulting Charles, Carl Piper and Lewenhaupt, Rehnskiöld decided to go on, saying, "In the name of God then, let us go forward". It was nearly 4:00 a.m. on 28 June in the Swedish calendar. Roos's column took the first two redoubts and killed every Russian inside, but by 4:30 its attack on the third had stalled.</p><p>Lewenhaupt's ten battalions on the right bypassed the first four redoubts, and Hamilton's cavalry on the left drove back the Russian cavalry. By 5:00 a.m. both wings were past the rear line of redoubts. Rehnskiöld halted the pursuit and ordered Lewenhaupt to withdraw west, where they waited two hours for Roos. Roos had six battalions, a third of the Swedish infantry, and after heavy losses led his remaining 1,500 of 2,600 men into the Yakovetski woods at 6:00 a.m. He was driven to an abandoned fort and surrendered at 9:30 a.m.</p><p>Unaware of this, the Swedes were faced by Peter's 42 battalions, 22,000 soldiers, who advanced from the camp with 55 three-pounder cannons and 32 guns on the ramparts. At 9:45 Rehnskiöld ordered the Swedish line forward. The Swedes were on the verge of a breakthrough, but their cavalry had not reformed in time, the left flank threw down its weapons and fled, and the Russians filled the gap. Menshikov's cavalry then attacked the Swedes from the rear.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/voltaire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/voltaire.jpg" alt="Voltaire" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolas_de_Largilli%C3%A8re_-_Portrait_de_Voltaire_(1694-1778)_en_1718_-_P208_-_mus%C3%A9e_Carnavalet_-_5_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nicolas de Largillière, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Charles ordered a retreat to the woods and gathered what troops he could, including the remnants of Creutz's detachment. With his litter destroyed, he rode on horseback and reached his baggage train at Pushkaryovka after 1:00 p.m., when the battle was over. He abandoned the siege of Poltava and retreated south at about 7:00 p.m. Lewenhaupt led the surviving Swedes and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, was pursued by Russian regular cavalry and 3,000 Kalmyk auxiliaries, and surrendered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulation-at-perevolochna">Perevolochna</a> on 11 July.</p><p>The Swedes lost many of their senior officers as prisoners: Field Marshal Rehnskiöld, Major Generals Schlippenbach, Stackelberg and Hamilton, Prince Maximilian Emanuel and Piper. Peter held a banquet in two large tents on the battlefield. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> assumed that his toast to the Swedish generals as masters of war was meant as a message to his own generals about disloyalty. Two mass graves held the Russian dead, 500 metres south-west of the Russian camp. Peter took many Swedes prisoner and sent them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>.</p><p>Charles and Mazepa escaped with about 1,500 men to Bendery in Moldavia, then controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Charles spent five years in exile there and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in December 1715. The high <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> was eventually paid off in a tangle of intrigue, which at one point involved a ransom of the Russian crown jewels, according to Charles's prison translator.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Much of what is known of the day comes from the reports of Swedish officers and a few named witnesses. For the weather, which was very hot and humid with the sun hidden by smoke from the Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> in the fort, the evidence is Charles himself and other Swedish officers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> is the source for the reason behind Peter's toast, and he only assumed it. The tale of the ransom of the Russian crown jewels comes from Charles's prison translator.</p><p>The day is timed to the half hour, from the arrival of the infantry at 2:30 a.m. to Roos's surrender at 9:30 a.m. and the Swedish advance at 9:45, and the date is 8 July 1709 in one calendar and 28 June in the Swedish one. Estimates of the Russian army’s strength run from 75,000 to 80,000. Two mass graves held the Russian dead, 500 metres south-west of their camp.</p><p>The battlefield has been listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> since 3 September 2009 as a monument of national historical significance.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-northern-war.jpg" alt="Great Northern War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Northern_War_Collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Imonoz, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Poltava is called the decisive and largest battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> and the greatest military catastrophe in Swedish history. It was a turning point in the war in favour of the anti-Swedish coalition, which was revived and attacked the weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> on several fronts with renewed vigour.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> lost its status as a European great power, and Poltava ended the time in which it was the dominant power in the Baltic region. After the war that position was taken over by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, and the battle also marked the beginning of Russian supremacy in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The battle is of crucial importance in the history of Sweden, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> alike.</p><p>For Mazepa and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a> the battle ended the attempt to join Charles against Peter, and Mazepa went into exile with the king. In Sweden the surrender at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulation-at-perevolochna">Perevolochna</a> three days later added to the disaster, since it took the rest of the army, apart from the 1,500 who escaped with the king and Mazepa. Charles returned to Sweden only in December 1715, after five years in exile. Since 2009 Ukraine has listed the battlefield as a monument of national historical significance.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Poltava on 8 July 1709 a Russian army under Peter I defeated the Swedes under Rehnskiöld, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> wounded, and the rest of the Swedish army surrendered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulation-at-perevolochna">Perevolochna</a> three days later. It is called the greatest military catastrophe in Swedish history and the start of Russian supremacy in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</em></p>

